      "God's justice is His unwavering commitment to do what is right — to honor good, to confront evil, to render to every creature what is due. He never grades on a curve. He never overlooks the wrong done to the weak. He never excuses Himself from His own standard. Abraham's question is the Bible's instinct: 'Shall not the Judge of all the earth do right?' (Gen 18:25). And the answer is yes — always, finally, perfectly.",
      "Why does this matter? Because the universe is, at the moment, riddled with injustice that cries out for an answer. The child who dies of preventable disease. The woman trafficked across borders. The whistleblower silenced. The genocide unpunished. The faithful believer killed for their faith. If there is no Judge, these wrongs simply pile up unanswered, and the universe is morally absurd. If there is a Judge, every wrong will be addressed, no matter how high the powers protected it. The justice of God is the only solid ground under any believer's prayer for the oppressed.",
      "But God's justice cuts in two directions, and the second direction is more uncomfortable. The same standard that condemns the trafficker also examines me. The same eyes that see Pharaoh's cruelty also see my pride, my gossip, my unforgiveness, my contempt. Romans 3 is unsparing — none is righteous, no, not one. Without the intervention of the cross, the just God's right verdict on every human, including the believer, would be guilty.",
      "And here is the gospel hidden inside the doctrine of justice. God did not, in the cross, suspend His justice to forgive us. He satisfied it. Christ took the just penalty of our sin in His body on the tree. The Father is, Paul writes, 'just and the justifier of the one who has faith in Jesus' (Rom 3:26). Mercy did not bend justice; mercy paid justice's price. The believer is forgiven not by overlooking but by transferring — our guilt placed on Christ, His righteousness given to us. Justice is therefore the believer's deepest comfort, not their fear.",
      "Practically: this week, two postures. First, trust the throne with the injustices you carry. The wrong done to you, to those you love, to the world — these are not forgotten. They are awaiting the Judge. Release them from your need to repay (Rom 12:19); the Lord will repay better than you ever could. Second, examine yourself in the light of the same justice. Where are you the perpetrator, not the victim, of injustice — in speech, in withholding, in small daily contempts? Confess. Receive the forgiveness that justice itself, paid for in Christ, makes possible.",
