    lesson: [
      "God knows everything. The technical term is omniscience. He knows every fact in the universe, every event past and future, every thought of every mind, every motive of every heart. He never learns, never forgets, never confuses one thing for another. The believer who is just learning the doctrine often finds it unsettling. The believer who has lived with it long finds it the deepest comfort in the universe.",
      "Why unsettling? Because the same God who knows the stars also knows you. He knows what you said about your friend behind their back. He knows the websites you visit when no one is looking. He knows the thoughts you have not been brave enough to confess. He knows the secret rooms you have locked from yourself. Hebrews 4:13 — 'no creature is hidden from his sight, but all are naked and exposed to the eyes of him to whom we must give account.' Read it slowly. There is no audit-proof corner of your life. There never has been.",
      "And yet. Read the next two verses of the same passage. 'Since then we have a great high priest who has passed through the heavens, Jesus the Son of God, let us hold fast our confession. For we do not have a high priest who is unable to sympathize with our weaknesses…' (Heb 4:14-15). The God who knows everything also became a man who knows everything we feel. He is not a distant scrutinizer; He is the High Priest who has lived under our skin. To be fully known by Him is also to be fully sympathized with by Him.",
      "And here is the deepest gospel: the God who knows you fully — every secret, every shame, every weakness — has chosen, in Christ, to love you. Most human relationships work in reverse. We hide our worst parts so we will be loved. We perform a curated self in hopes the real one will not be discovered. The terror is that if they really knew, they would not love us. With God, that fear is reversed. He really knows. He really loves. The two are not in tension. They are settled, in Christ.",
      "Practically: this week, two postures. First, stop hiding. There is no hidden room from Him; the only thing hiding does is hide you from yourself. Bring one shadowed corner of your life into His light. Confess what you have been pretending He does not see. Receive His forgiveness. Stop carrying what He has already weighed. Second, rest in being known. Many believers go through whole lives feeling unknown — by spouses, by friends, by the Church. You are not unknown. The One whose knowledge matters most knows you fully, by name, with delight. Let that sink into the unknown places. It is breath.",
    ],
