    lesson: [
      "God is good. Not just powerful — good. Not just true — good. Not just holy — good. The Bible's testimony is unanimous: 'You are good, and what you do is good' (Ps 119:68). 'The Lord is good to all, and his mercy is over all that he has made' (Ps 145:9). 'Every good and perfect gift is from above' (Jas 1:17). Whatever is genuinely good in your life today — your friendships, the colors of the autumn, the smell of bread, the laughter of a child, a song that moved you, the breath in your lungs — came from Him. He is the source.",
      "Two implications. First, the believer's posture toward life should be gratitude. Not the strained kind that tries to convince itself it should be grateful, but the honest kind that sees a good gift and names its Giver. Cultivate this. Each day, identify three specific good things and thank the Father for them. Over months, this practice rewires the soul. Believers who keep this discipline tend to be remarkable people — quiet, glad, generous, unhurried.",
      "Second, His goodness is the answer to the problem of evil. This sounds backwards. Surely the problem of evil is the strongest case against God's goodness? But notice — we only recognize evil as evil because we know goodness, and we only know goodness because there is a Good. An atheist cosmos has no category for evil; it has only things-we-prefer-not-to-happen. The Christian's anguish over evil is precisely a measure of how much they have tasted goodness. The God who is good has not, in this age, removed all evil; He has done something harder. He has entered it Himself, on a cross. The world is not yet fully right. He has not yet made it right. He will.",
      "But God's goodness is, even now, intensely personal. Psalm 23 — 'Surely goodness and mercy shall follow me all the days of my life' (v. 6). The Hebrew word for 'follow' here is closer to 'pursue.' Goodness chases the believer. The same Father who feeds the sparrows feeds you. The same God who hung the stars knows when your knees ache. Romans 8:32 is the supreme proof: 'He who did not spare His own Son but gave Him up for us all — how will He not also with Him graciously give us all things?' If He gave the costliest gift, what will He not give in the smaller ones?",
      "Practically: this week, practice tasting. Slow down at one meal each day to actually taste what is on your plate, with thanksgiving. Walk somewhere outdoors and notice three pieces of creation that preach His goodness. Write down three goods received from Him each evening. Watch your soul slowly turn toward Him as your default posture. The believer who has tasted that the Lord is good walks the world unable to forget it.",
    ],
